WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:50.229
Jiro Horikoshi was the most brilliant Japanese
aeronautic engineer of his time.

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:54.520
He grew up dreaming of bringing Japan into
an age where people could travel the world

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.520
in airplanes.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.119
But he was born in a time when his country
was at war, and the only use for his talent

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:02.920
was to make bombers during World War II.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.150
Horikoshi designed the famous A6M Zero Bomber.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:11.670
This was considered to be the most successful
dog fighter of its time, and the plane would

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.810
eventually become associated with the infamous
Kamikazes.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:21.000
On today’s Biographics, we have the story
of Jiro Horikoshi’s life, and the impact

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.310
his creation had on the world.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:31.530
Head in the Clouds
Before we get started with today’s story,

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.570
we’re going to do something a bit different.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:38.379
I want you to think for a moment about the
dream you had as a child.

00:02:38.379 --> 00:02:41.050
Maybe you wanted to be an astronaut, artist,
or teacher.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:46.400
This passion we all have when we are young
often dies off as we get older, because the

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.440
powers-at-be try to place obstacles in our
way.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:55.640
For Jiro Horikoshi, the thing that always
filled him with childhood wonder was airplanes.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.770
Born in 1903, Japan was slowly transitioning
from living an ancient lifestyle to adopting

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.250
modern technology that had been created in
Europe.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.780
In Jiro’s home town of Fujioka in Gunma
Prefecture, people were still traveling on

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.660
dirt roads, and using oxen to plow their fields.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.099
But Jiro looked up to the sky.

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:14.910
Sometimes, if he was very lucky, he could
see an airplane flying overhead.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:19.090
Even if he couldn’t touch or see it, he
knew that there was a whole world out there

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.879
him to explore off the islands of Japan.

00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:26.250
The idea that a man could fly seemed like
magic, and he wanted to become a pilot.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:29.859
However, he was nearsighted from a young age,
and began wearing glasses.

00:03:29.859 --> 00:03:33.290
He could not become a pilot because of his
eyesight, but he learned that it was still

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:36.900
possible to design planes, even if he could
never fly one.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.730
The only way Jiro could learn more about airplanes
was to find European and American magazines

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:42.730
on the subject.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:47.310
Japanese translations did not exist, so he
would have to get a dictionary and go through

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.430
the articles word-by-word in order to understand
them.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:53.959
This was the first generation of people in
the world to experience electricity, and Japan

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:55.810
was still running by candlelight.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:01.480
Jiro Horikoshi’s mind was already traveling
the world and learning to respect other cultures.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:05.959
At the time, one of the most respected aeronautical
engineers in the world was an Italian man

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:08.300
named Giovanni Battista Caproni.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:12.799
He was famous for constantly pushing the envelope
on innovation, and testing the limits of what

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:14.310
was possible in airplane design.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:18.620
A silent film was circulated around the world
showing Caproni’s ridiculous attempt at

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:22.710
trying to make a “flying boat”, which
was a passenger plane with multiple layers

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:23.800
of wings.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.740
Even the mere concept of a passenger plane
was incredible at the time, because small

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.060
aircraft could only carry one or two people.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.120
The idea that something so heavy could fly
through the air with dozens of people inside

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.120
seemed impossible.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.680
In today’s world, we truly do take it for
granted how easy it is for an entire family

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.990
to go on holiday.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:47.770
Jiro Horikoshi would have seen Caproni’s
attempts to make magnificent planes.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:52.719
Caproni was truly a legend among aspiring
engineers, and young Jiro began sketching

00:04:52.719 --> 00:04:57.090
his childish designs of the planes in his
imagination.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:05.180
Living the Dream
Lucky for Jiro Horikoshi, Tokyo University

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.930
opened up a brand new aviation program at
their school.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:12.240
He traveled to the big city to join a group
of young men who were just as eager to learn

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.790
about engineering as he was.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:17.901
Finally, Jiro had a group of friends who were
just as passionate about planes, and most

00:05:17.901 --> 00:05:20.130
of them would go on to become his future colleagues.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:24.479
In Tokyo, people were finally traveling by
trains and tram cars, and the city was a blend

00:05:24.479 --> 00:05:27.980
of traditional Japanese style and European
influences.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:32.580
For a boy who grew up in the middle of nowhere,
this would have been truly spectacular.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.660
He began taking classes in Calculus and Physics,
and learned how to elevate his sketches for

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:38.470
airplane design blueprints.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:42.889
For a while, things were looking up, and he
was on the path to making his dream a reality.

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:49.250
But on September 1, 1923, Jiro was 20-years
old when Tokyo was struck with The Great Kanto

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:50.250
Earthquake.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:55.719
It only took years for Japan to finally install
their modern innovations, but only a few seconds

00:05:55.719 --> 00:05:57.280
for it all to crumble around them.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.940
The trains and trams were derailed from their
tracks, and buildings were destroyed.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:05.889
The earthquake happened at noon, so almost
everyone in the city was cooking their lunch

00:06:05.889 --> 00:06:08.889
over an open flame all at the exact same time.

00:06:08.889 --> 00:06:13.710
This caused a series of firestorms like nothing
we have ever seen in modern times.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:17.539
Thousands of people attempted to take shelter
in the army clothing depot, only for them

00:06:17.539 --> 00:06:23.500
to become sitting ducks in the pathway of
a fire tornado that killed 38,000 people.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:27.569
Since the earthquake destroyed the water mains,
it took two days to put out the flames.

00:06:27.569 --> 00:06:32.610
Clouds of black smoke filled the skies of
Japan, and it could be seen from miles away.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.729
The earthquake was followed by a tsunami,
and several after-shocks.

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:39.900
Thousands of people were dead, and nearly
2 million became homeless.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.330
Just like that, the Japanese people were back
to living in the dark ages.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.680
What followed was a time of great paranoia.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:50.080
Racist sentiments against Koreans and Chinese
people lead to rumors that their limited resources

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.539
were being poisoned, which lead to massacres
of anyone who was non-Japanese.

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:57.800
Without proper housing or health care, disease
began to spread, as well.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:02.030
When people are struggling to have their basic
needs met, there is no room for higher education

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:03.030
or innovation.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:08.470
There would be no industrial revolution for
Jiro Horikoshi and the other young engineers.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:13.030
During the fire, students at Tokyo University
scrambled to save their precious aeronautical

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:14.280
engineering books from the flames.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.069
Most of the students had to bury their friends
and family.

00:07:18.069 --> 00:07:21.790
For a long time, no one was worrying about
studying math and science.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:24.800
It was purely about surviving to live another
day.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.710
It would take years for the city to fully
recover from the natural disaster.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:33.460
Many of the original class either died, or
had to give up their dreams in order to work.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:38.060
Jiro was fortunate enough to have family back
in Fujioka, so they didn’t lose everything

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:39.060
they owned.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:45.319
When the school reopened, he was able to return
to Tokyo and complete his degree.

00:07:45.319 --> 00:07:51.530
Working for Mitsubishi
After graduating from Tokyo University, Jiro

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:56.330
Horikoshi got his first engineering job working
for Mitsubishi when he was 24 years old.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:59.740
Today, you might recognize the name for being
a famous car manufacturer.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:01.410
Turns out they have been around since 1870.

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:06.819
At the time, the corporation was called Mitsubishi
Internal Combustion Engine Company Limited.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:11.310
Their main competition was the Nakajima Aircraft
Company, and the two were rivals in the fight

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:13.620
to win contracts from the Imperial Navy.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:18.710
Unlike the audacious designs of Giovanni Capronni,
no one in Japan was thinking about developing

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:20.949
passenger planes to take people on holiday.

00:08:20.949 --> 00:08:26.430
Obviously, the Navy’s main concern was making
sure Imperial Japan could be just as strong,

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.360
if not stronger than their enemies.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:33.210
For his first assignment, Jiro Horikoshi was
asked to work on an existing team that was

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:39.000
tasked with rebuilding The Mitsubishi 1MF9,
which had been nicknamed “The Falcon”.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.250
It had been commissioned by the Imperial Japanese
Navy.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:45.070
A crowd of people stood to watch the Falcon
take its first flight, only to witness it

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:49.720
completely fall apart after the pilot attempted
to reach maximum speeds.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:53.680
Engineering companies like Mitsubishi needed
government contracts in order to survive.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:58.280
The higher-ups in the Imperial Japanese Navy
told all of the engineers that they needed

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:03.850
faster planes in their ongoing conflict with
China during The Second Sino-Japanese War,

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.720
which overlapped with World War II.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:09.380
If they did not satisfy the Imperial Navy,
Jiro and his friends would all be out of a

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:12.520
job, and their nation may lose the war with
China.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:16.620
He worked day and night doing maths and sketching
out designs.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:20.370
Most of the airplanes created by Japanese
engineers were copies of ones that had already

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:21.740
existed elsewhere.

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:26.740
Creating a new plane design that has never
existed before is incredibly difficult and

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:27.740
expensive to make.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:33.140
After years of trial and error, Jiro finally
created a blueprint of a completely innovative

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:37.319
plane that was worthy of production when he
was 29 years old.

00:09:37.319 --> 00:09:43.620
This was the Mitsubishi 1MF10, and it went
in development from 1932 to 1933.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:47.020
It was a monoplane, which means that it only
had one wing instead of two.

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:52.010
However, after two failed tests, it was clear
that more adjustments had to be made in his

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.010
calculations.

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:58.530
Two years later, in 1935, Jiro created the
A5M, and it became the world’s first carrier

00:09:58.530 --> 00:09:59.530
monoplane.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:00.690
It was revolutionary for the time.

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:03.890
However, his innovation was ignored outside
of Japan.

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:08.420
In an issue of Flying &amp; Popular Aviation magazine,
one article said, “Japan’s military planes

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:13.689
are so poor in comparison with other powers
… They have not yet gotten beyond merely

00:10:13.689 --> 00:10:15.510
imitating what others have done.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:20.650
Someday, perhaps, the Japanese will have accumulated
enough experience in a mechanical way to catch

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:23.750
up, but that day will not come soon.”

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:26.650
Actually, that day came much sooner than anyone
expected.

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:31.930
By 1940, Jiro began working on the design
of the next model, the A6M.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:36.370
It was dubbed the “model number 00”, or
simply, the “Zero Bomber”.

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:42.590
It reached speeds of 500/km per hour, and
it was equipped with two 7.7 mm machine guns,

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:44.889
as well as two 20 mm cannons.

00:10:44.889 --> 00:10:50.980
It could fly faster than any American plane,
and it suddenly put Japan at a technological

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.170
advantage over the Allies.

00:10:53.170 --> 00:11:01.330
Pearl Harbor, and World War II
When the Japanese Imperial Navy saw the demonstration

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:06.160
of the speed and agility of the zero bombers,
they were singing high praises for Mitsubishi’s

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:08.770
work, and Jiro’s genius mind.

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:12.899
In China, these Zero Bombers were able to
completely dominate their enemy’s outdated

00:11:12.899 --> 00:11:14.690
biplanes.

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:17.050
They were absolutely no match for Japan.

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:21.870
However, none of the Mitsubishi engineers
knew about the planned attack on America.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:23.240
We all know what happens next.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:27.010
The United States finally decided to enter
the conflict of World War II.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:30.940
Just like Icarus, the Japanese Empire flew
far too close to the sun, and its resources

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:36.950
were stretched too thin, but these Zero Bombers
gave the Emperor a sense of new-found power.

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.290
For so long, the Allies had underestimated
Japan.

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:42.180
This Zero Bomber took everyone by surprise.

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:46.400
In fact, even Allied Intelligence operatives
had ignored any information regarding the

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:50.680
creation of Jiro Horikoshi’s new airplanes,
because they simply could not believe that

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:55.680
little old Japan was capable of developing
such an advanced bomber, especially when their

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.279
aeronautic industry was still so young.

00:11:58.279 --> 00:12:02.610
Mitsubishi continued to pump out more and
more Zero bombers for the Imperial Navy, because

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.800
they were the only thing helping them stay
ahead of the Americans.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:10.279
For a while, the US was truly terrified of
the speeds, and couldn’t keep up.

00:12:10.279 --> 00:12:14.300
Eventually, the US navy recovered a fully
intact Zero Bomber, and they were able to

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:17.550
examine Jiro’s design and isolate its weaknesses.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:21.730
They realized that the metal on the plane
was so thin, all it took was just a few shots

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:24.420
to the fuel tank, and it would explode.

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:27.839
Even though it could reach high speeds, it
became more difficult to maneuver and dodge

00:12:27.839 --> 00:12:29.850
quickly when it was going too fast.

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:33.790
The Americans finally figured out how to take
down this nearly-unstoppable plane.

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:37.950
Because of this, Japan was losing their best
pilots, and they were forced to send men who

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:39.449
had little-to-no experience.

00:12:39.449 --> 00:12:43.389
Eventually, the Americans could easily take
down the Zero Bombers in a matter of minutes,

00:12:43.389 --> 00:12:46.709
and there was no time to create an even better
plane to replace it.

00:12:46.709 --> 00:12:50.839
The only maneuver that gave Japan any hope
was the “Kamikaze”, which was when young

00:12:50.839 --> 00:12:55.920
pilots were instructed to commit suicide by
flying directly into the American ships at

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:56.920
high speeds.

00:12:56.920 --> 00:13:00.830
In anti-Japanese propaganda films, they claimed
that these men were so brainwashed by their

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:05.900
idolization of the Emperor, that they were
terrorist who were willing to go on a suicide

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:06.900
mission.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:10.850
These rumors only fueled this idea that Japanese
people were all crazy, and needed to be stopped

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:12.350
at all costs.

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:16.640
According to one of the Kamikaze pilots who
actually survived, Saburo Sakai, none of those

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:20.600
men were brainwashed, and they weren’t trying
to kill themselves on behalf of the emperor.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.380
They were desperately trying to protect the
people they loved, because they felt that

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:26.820
maybe their own death would help keep others
alive.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:30.690
Sakai said, “Even if you don't tell him
to crash into something, putting a kid with

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:35.190
only about 20 hours flight time into a plane
and telling him to take on U.S. pilots is

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:38.940
just as much a suicidal tactic as being a
kamikaze.

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:42.010
But let me tell you, all that stuff you read
about dying for the emperor?

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:43.149
that's all crap.

00:13:43.149 --> 00:13:47.279
They were thinking about their mother and
their family, just like anybody else.”

00:13:47.279 --> 00:13:51.199
In reality, “Kamikaze” was never part
of Jiro Horikoshi’s plan.

00:13:51.199 --> 00:13:56.360
He had actually designed these planes to last
for years, so that they could be re-used again

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:57.360
and again.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:01.720
In fact, he even incorporated a flotation
device to release on impact, which ensured

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:04.300
that the plane would not sink after crashing
into the ocean.

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:07.759
He did everything possible to ensure the safety
of the pilots.

00:14:07.759 --> 00:14:12.160
The idea was to make a plane that was so incredibly
fast, the enemy could not keep up, and they

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:15.860
could get in and out before putting themselves
in any unnecessary danger.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:20.000
Just like with everything else, it was the
military’s decision to push men to suicide,

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:21.000
not his.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.090
Jiro later wrote in his diary how horrified
he was by the whole situation.

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:28.740
“When we awoke on the morning of December
8, 1941, we found ourselves — without any

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:31.720
foreknowledge — to be embroiled in war...

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:35.410
Since then, the majority of us who had truly
understood the awesome industrial strength

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:39.500
of the United States never really believed
that Japan would win this war.

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:43.630
We were convinced that surely our government
had in mind some diplomatic measures which

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:47.950
would bring the conflict to a halt before
the situation became catastrophic for Japan.

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:52.769
But now, bereft of any strong government move
to seek a diplomatic way out, we are being

00:14:52.769 --> 00:14:53.800
driven to doom.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:55.550
Japan is being destroyed.

00:14:55.550 --> 00:15:00.399
I cannot do [anything] other but to blame
the military hierarchy and the blind politicians

00:15:00.399 --> 00:15:05.209
in power for dragging Japan into this hellish
cauldron of defeat.”

00:15:05.209 --> 00:15:08.800
If the war wasn’t bad enough already, there
was yet another natural disaster.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:13.790
In 1944, a major earthquake forced Mitsubishi
to halt their productions of Zero Bombers,

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:17.560
which put Japan at an even greater disadvantage
against the Allies.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:20.370
Many of the buildings of the factory were
destroyed.

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:24.680
The engineers were relocated to a school building
while the factory was repaired.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:29.960
Jiro Horikoshi was growing more and more depressed
and anxious about the fact that his planes

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:34.290
were causing so much destruction, and that
he was indirectly involved with the destruction

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:35.860
of the Japanese Empire.

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:39.470
He fell ill with pleurisy, and this forced
him to take time off of work.

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:43.019
That same year, Tokyo and Nagoya were being
bombed by the Americans.

00:15:43.019 --> 00:15:44.829
Jiro was too sick to evacuate.

00:15:44.829 --> 00:15:49.500
So he sent his five children, brother-in-law,
and his elderly mother to live in his family

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:50.950
home in Gunma.

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:54.790
Since it was in such a remote farming community,
there was far less danger there.

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:56.139
His wife stayed in Nagoya.

00:15:56.139 --> 00:15:59.639
She refused to leave his side, and nursed
him back to health.

00:15:59.639 --> 00:16:03.420
When he finally recovered and returned to
work, he saw the damage that had been done

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:04.420
to the city in his absence.

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:09.459
He wrote, “For the first time, I really
saw the effects of the incendiary raids on

00:16:09.459 --> 00:16:10.459
Nagoya.

00:16:10.459 --> 00:16:14.290
The city is a wasteland, charred and unspeakably
desolate.

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:19.750
My former factory is a ghostly, steel-ribbed
wreck, shattered by bombs and torn apart by

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:20.960
the dispersal crews.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:23.740
It is hard to believe that all this is true.

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:25.639
I knew that soon I would be well.

00:16:25.639 --> 00:16:28.750
Strangely, though, I had little desire to
return to work.

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:33.410
The impression of the shattered city and the
wrecked factories will not leave me.”

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:37.560
After spending only a week back at work, Jiro
Horikoshi realized that he truly could not

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:38.560
take it anymore.

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:41.199
He didn’t want to participate in engineering
weapons of war.

00:16:41.199 --> 00:16:45.519
His wife accompanied him back to Gunma, where
he could live far away from the violence.

00:16:45.519 --> 00:16:49.540
Even then, he could still hear the distant
sound of far-off-bombs being dropped on Japanese

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:50.769
cities.

00:16:50.769 --> 00:16:59.459
The End of the Empire
In 1945, Tokyo was firebombed, and the Americans

00:16:59.459 --> 00:17:02.680
killed between 80,000 to 130,000 people.

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:07.280
The Japanese were convinced even more than
ever that the sacrifice of a few men in order

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:09.530
to save thousands was completely necessary.

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:14.169
A 22-year old pilot named Kiyoshi Ogawa wrote
a letter to his mother after he found out

00:17:14.169 --> 00:17:16.000
that he had been assigned a Kamikaze mission.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:19.829
He wrote, “Looking back, when I think of
your raising me in your arms for more than

00:17:19.829 --> 00:17:22.750
twenty years, I am filled with a sense of
gratitude.

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:26.470
I truly believe that no one else has lived
a happier life than me.

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:32.010
I am resolved to repay your kindness...I always
and forever will be living near you.”

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:36.950
Together with Seizō Yasunori, they flew their
Zero bombers directly into an American aircraft

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:41.810
carrier USS Bunker Hill, killing and wounding
hundreds of men.

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:46.750
Even though the deaths in Pearl Harbor and
USS Bunker Hill was a drop in the bucket compared

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:51.190
to the damage that the Americans had done
to Tokyo, the US continued to dehumanized

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:55.669
Japanese people to the point where it suddenly
became justifiable to drop the atomic bombs

00:17:55.669 --> 00:18:05.139
on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in 1945, killing
an additional 129,000 to 226,000 people.

00:18:05.139 --> 00:18:10.290
Mitsubishi manufactured a total of 10,400
Zero Bombers, and by the end of the war, they

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:11.670
were all destroyed.

00:18:11.670 --> 00:18:15.960
Thousands of men were killed in their prime
because of the greed of a few leaders in power.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:20.750
Jiro Horikoshi had to live the rest of his
life knowing that he was responsible for making

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:23.070
this plane that lead to so many deaths.

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:29.899
In a different time and place, his mind could
have been used for greater things.

00:18:29.899 --> 00:18:36.899
Legacy
After Japan was occupied by the Allies, Mitsubishi

00:18:36.899 --> 00:18:39.900
was forced to disband its operations in 1947.

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:45.060
Jiro Horikoshi stopped designing planes, and
became a professor at the Tokyo University

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:47.290
Institute of Space and Aeronautics.

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:51.930
Finally, he was able to teach his passion
to younger men who were growing up in a time

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:52.930
of peace.

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:57.160
Even though they lost the war, Jiro Horikoshi
proved to the world that Japanese people truly

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:01.770
could invent new and innovative technology,
just like the western world.

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:06.970
He lived to see Japan develop into the industrialized
society that he had wanted for so long.

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:11.290
In the 1970’s, he finally achieved his dream
of boarding a trans-continental passenger

00:19:11.290 --> 00:19:13.559
plane from Tokyo to New York City.

00:19:13.559 --> 00:19:17.910
He lived to be 78 years old, and died of pneumonia
in 1982.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:22.850
Now that the nation was entering a time of
peace, Japan could finally enter their technological

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:23.850
renaissance.

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:27.400
Today, when we think of the places in the
world that have the most advanced technological

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:31.580
breakthroughs in science and robotics, they
one of the first places we think of.

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:37.150
In 2013, the Oscar-winning Japanese director
Hayao Miyazaki premiered an animated film

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:41.440
about Jiro Horikoshi’s life called The Wind
Rises.

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:45.620
This was the last movie Miyazaki made after
announcing his retirement, so it became Japan’s

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:47.880
highest grossing film of that year.

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:52.039
There is also a documentary of the behind-the-scenes
making of the movie, called The Kingdom of

00:19:52.039 --> 00:19:53.070
Dreams and Madness.

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:55.390
Hayao Miyazaki wasn’t just the artistic
director.

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:56.750
He also wrote the screenplay.

00:19:56.750 --> 00:20:00.430
While he could find all of the facts about
the sequence of events Jiro Horikoshi’s

00:20:00.430 --> 00:20:04.660
life, there was little known about his relationships,
or his personality.

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:09.030
Hayao Miyazaki’s father, Katsuji, was also
an aeronautical engineer.

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:12.669
By being born just a few years later, Katsuji
Miyazaki was spared the fate of having his

00:20:12.669 --> 00:20:14.789
designs used as weapons of war.

00:20:14.789 --> 00:20:19.830
In the moments where Jiro’s personal life
was a mystery, Miyazaki filled in the gaps

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:21.900
with the memories of his father.

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:26.350
He presents the idea that when two people
share the same dream, they are connected in

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:28.510
a way that transcends time and space.

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:34.090
No matter where or when you were born, they
can always understand one another’s passion.

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:38.650
While accepting an award for the film on Horikoshi’s
life, Miyazaki said that he was incredibly

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:43.549
privileged to have had a 50-year career during
a time of peace, because it meant that he

00:20:43.549 --> 00:20:47.190
have the luxury to focus on making beautiful
things.

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:51.210
In the end, one can see Jiro Horikoshi’s
legacy, and believe that he is responsible

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:52.510
for thousands of deaths.

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:57.140
Or, they can choose to see the young boy who
had the passion for airplanes, only for the

00:20:57.140 --> 00:21:23.679
forces of evil to turn something beautiful
into a vehicle of destruction.

